<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/intermediate/figures-of-speech">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Figures of speech are <strong>systematic deviations from plain language that do work plain
    language can't</strong>: compress comparisons, structure persuasion, and perform emotion in
    sound. They are as old as rhetoric (Aristotle catalogued them) and as current as every
    headline, slogan and song lyric you'll meet today.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="tropes shift meaning, schemes arrange sound, metaphors run cognition" widget="equationStep" config={figuresCfg} />

  <h2>Why rhetoric is a survival skill</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Framing analysis:</strong> "death tax" vs "estate tax" — same law, different metaphor, different poll numbers. Naming the figure disarms it (the statistics ladder's sibling skill).</li>
    <li><strong>Euphemism and dysphemism:</strong> "collateral damage", "passed away", "downsizing" — the figure chosen tells you the speaker's project.</li>
    <li><strong>Literary figures do cognitive work:</strong> metaphor maps one domain onto another (ARGUMENT IS WAR, IDEAS ARE FOOD) — the way we talk reveals how we think (Lakoff & Johnson, 1980).</li>
    <li><strong>The catalogue is ancient:</strong> the Rhetorica ad Herennium (~90 BCE) listed dozens; every generation adds names for new distortions. Learning ~20 covers most of what you'll meet.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Aristotle, Rhetoric; Lakoff & Johnson, Metaphors We Live By (1980); Corbett & Connors, Classical Rhetoric for the Modern Student.</p>
</TopicPage>
